# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Key Problems in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but **does not wake `WaitOne()`** — only interrupts `Sleep`/`Join` |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | Race condition: multiple `Enqueue` calls before `Dequeue` → signal lost, items skipped |
| Raw `Thread` + `while(true)` | No cooperative cancellation — hard to shut down cleanly |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Clean shutdown — expected
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _processTask is null) return;

        _channel.Writer.Complete();                         // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline

        try { await _processTask; }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<string>` | Thread-safe producer/consumer with built-in backpressure; no lost signals |
| `Thread` + `while(true)` | `Task.Run` + `await foreach` | Uses thread pool; cooperative cancellation; no blocked threads |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | `Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8 |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Graceful drain — processes remaining items before stopping |
| `IDisposable` (implicit) | `IAsyncDisposable` | Proper async cleanup pattern |

## Common Pitfalls

1. **`Thread.Abort()` silent failures** — Code compiles but throws at runtime. Search your codebase:
   ```bash
   grep -rn "\.Abort()\|\.Interrupt()" --include="*.cs"
   ```

2. **Lost signals** — The original code loses items when `Enqueue` is called multiple times before `RunLoop` dequeues. `Channel` eliminates this entirely.

3. **Sync-over-async trap** — Don't call `StopAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult()` from a UI/ASP.NET context. If you need a sync `Stop()`:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop()
   {
       _channel.Writer.Complete();
       _cts?.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
       _processTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult(); // OK from background/console only
   }
   ```

4. **Bounded vs Unbounded** — For production, prefer `CreateBounded<string>(capacity)` to apply backpressure and prevent OOM under load.
